  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the structural feature of whether a lottery contains a sure outcome (100% probability result), but the individual does not consider certainty or the presence/absence of sure outcomes at all. Instead, the individual emphasizes the size of the prize, the subjective assessment of probability (45% seen as 'very high'), and personal risk tolerance. Since the individual does not engage with the concept of sure outcomes, and the reason’s key criterion (preference for lotteries without sure outcomes) is not reflected in the deliberation, there is no evidence the reason was used. Moreover, since neither lottery has a sure outcome, even if the individual had considered this, the reason would not lead to a preference—further distancing it from the actual justification given. The mismatch in both relevant information and decision rule leads to a low assessment.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

