  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates whether each lottery contains a 'sure outcome'—that is, an outcome that occurs with 100% probability. In Lottery A, there is a 55% chance of 0 Euros and a 45% chance of -6000 Euros; no outcome occurs with certainty, so there is no sure outcome. In Lottery B, there is a 90% chance of -3000 Euros and a 10% chance of 0 Euros; again, no outcome occurs with 100% probability, so there is also no sure outcome. Therefore, both lotteries lack a sure outcome. According to the decision reason, the preferred lottery is the one *without* a sure outcome. Since neither lottery has a sure outcome, the reason would be indifferent between them or, depending on interpretation, could prefer both equally. However, the key information—the presence or absence of a sure outcome—can be derived from the lotteries’ structures.",
