{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on the presence of a sure outcome (i.e., an outcome that occurs with 100% probability) in each lottery. A sure outcome would mean one of the lotteries guarantees a specific payoff regardless of chance. In Lottery A, the outcomes are 6000 Euros (25%) and 0 Euros (75%) — no outcome occurs with 100% probability, so there is no sure outcome. In Lottery B, the outcomes are 4000 (25%), 2000 (25%), and 0 (50%) — again, no outcome occurs with certainty. Therefore, neither lottery contains a sure outcome. The decision reason prefers the lottery *without* a sure outcome. Since both lotteries lack a sure outcome, the reason would be indifferent between them, or possibly prefer both equally under its rule. However, the applicability of the reason is questionable because the condition it evaluates (presence of a sure outcome) is absent in both options.",
